Excellent question 👌 You’re thinking in the right direction. Let’s carefully break it down.

🔹 Factorial definition

By mathematics:

0! = 1
1! = 1


Both 0! and 1! are defined as 1.

🔹 Why do we use (n == 0 || n == 1) in recursion?

When writing recursion, we need a base case where the recursion stops.

If we only use n == 1, then calling fact(0) would cause infinite recursion:

fact(0) → 0 * fact(-1) → fact(-2) → fact(-3) ...


which is wrong.

Including n == 0 ensures the function works even if input is 0.

🔹 Example

If we only use if (n == 1) return 1;

fact(5) works fine, because it will eventually reach fact(1).

But fact(0) will fail (infinite recursion).

If we use if (n == 0 || n == 1) return 1;

Both fact(5) and fact(0) work correctly.

🔹 Rule of thumb

If you know input will always be ≥ 1, then n == 1 is enough.

If you want a universal factorial function (that works for 0! too), use n == 0 || n == 1.

fact(0) → 0 * fact(-1) → infinite recursion → StackOverflowError


